  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nThe low pressure system that has been resulting in the cooler, \nmore seasonable temperatures is moving eastwards into the \nnortheastern CONUS. Across the southwestern CONUS, a large \nupper-level low embedded within the subtropical jet is slowly \nmoving eastward into New Mexico and west Texas, resulting in the\nridge in the western CONUS moving into the central CONUS. The \neastward moving ridge is causing warm air to begin advecting \ninto our region today and over the next couple of days as the \ncore of the polar jet remains north of our area. Temperatures \ntonight look to be a few degrees warmer than last night, so most\nof the area will be staying just above freezing. However, a few\nareas well outside of the Chicago metro could see temperatures \ndrop to right around the freezing point. It appears winds will \nincrease tomorrow as a subtle trough with the polar jet will \nincrease the rate of WAA in our region tomorrow. High \ntemperatures will rise above normal tomorrow through Tuesday \nwith highs in the low to possibly mid 60s during those days.\n\nWith clear skies tonight along with persistent warm air \nadvection, low stratus and perhaps some regions of fog could \ndevelop later tonight as low-level moistening occurs. If any \nfog does form, it appears it will remain rather localized and \nfocused west- northwest of most of the Chicago metro area \nthrough early Sunday morning.\n\nLooking ahead to later in the week, deterministic and ensemble \nmodels are all showing a substantial cool down next week. An \nupper- level low looks to form in east-central Canada early to \nmid next week, then move southwards into our region, arriving \nhere later Wednesday and into Thursday. It appears pop chances \nwill remain rather low during the arrival of the upper- level \nlow, deterministic models show the possibility of lake effect \nprecipitation beginning later Wednesday and into Thursday. \nDepending on the level of cold air that gets advected into our \nregion, there is a possibility of someone near downtown Chicago \nand northwest Indiana getting their first snowflakes of the \nseason.\n\nParsons/KJB",
